+# Wire Rod Repository Governance
+
+This file contains mandatory repository-level rules for every human developer
+and every AI/coding agent working on this project.
+
+Read this file before making any non-trivial repository change.
+
+## 1. Canonical repository structure
+
+The canonical locations are:
+
+```text
+doc/
+  requirements/   approved requirements and business rules
+  design/         architecture, algorithm and detailed design
+  decisions/      approved technical/product decisions
+  status/         implementation status, handoff and validation records
+
+dataset/
+  labels/         GT, manifests, pair definitions and dataset metadata
+  *.ply           benchmark/generated point-cloud frames
+```
+
+Rules:
+
+- Formal requirement/design/decision documents MUST live under `doc/`.
+- Dataset PLY files MUST live directly under `dataset/`.
+- Dataset labels/GT/metadata MUST live under `dataset/labels/`.
+- Do not recreate legacy root directories `docs/`, `design/` or `labels/`.
+- Do not place PLY files in the repository root.
+
+## 2. Protected core assets
+
+The following paths are PROTECTED CORE ASSETS:
+
+```text
+doc/requirements/**
+doc/design/**
+doc/decisions/**
+doc/GOVERNANCE.md
+AGENTS.md
+```
+
+Protected assets are specification assets, not implementation notes.
+
+They define what the system is expected to do and MUST NOT be silently changed
+to match whatever the current code happens to do.
+
+## 3. Owner-approval gate
+
+A protected asset may be modified only when the project owner gives explicit
+approval for that document/change in the current task or conversation.
+
+Approval is scoped. It does not automatically authorize unrelated protected
+document changes.
+
+Do NOT infer document-update approval from requests such as:
+
+- fix this bug;
+- implement this feature;
+- refactor this module;
+- make the tests pass;
+- update the code;
+- the current implementation already behaves this way.
+
+If approval is absent or ambiguous, leave protected documents unchanged.
+
+## 4. Code/document conflict protocol
+
+When code, tests, configuration or runtime behavior disagrees with a protected
+document:
+
+1. DO NOT silently update the protected document.
+2. DO NOT treat current code as the new requirement automatically.
+3. Report the conflict to the project owner.
+4. Identify:
+   - document path and relevant section;
+   - code files/functions involved;
+   - documented behavior;
+   - implemented/proposed behavior;
+   - practical impact;
+   - recommended resolution.
+5. State clearly:
+
+   `DOCUMENT UPDATE REQUIRES OWNER APPROVAL`
+
+6. Wait for explicit approval before changing the protected document.
+
+Use this report header:
+
+```text
+[DOCUMENT CONFLICT]
+```
+
+## 5. Source-of-truth rule
+
+For previously approved requirements/design decisions, protected documentation
+is the baseline source of truth until the project owner explicitly approves a
+change.
+
+Code does not become the specification merely because it was implemented first.
+
+If a requested code change intentionally creates a temporary mismatch, report
+that mismatch before finalizing the work. Do not hide it.
+
+## 6. Mandatory change workflow
+
+For every non-trivial change:
+
+1. Read `AGENTS.md`.
+2. Read relevant protected documents under `doc/`.
+3. Compare the request with the documented requirement/design.
+4. Report any conflict before editing protected assets.
+5. Implement only the approved scope.
+6. Run relevant tests.
+7. Provide a unified diff.
+8. Report documentation consistency using exactly one of:
+
+```text
+DOCUMENT STATUS: CONSISTENT
+DOCUMENT STATUS: CONFLICT FOUND
+DOCUMENT STATUS: UPDATE APPROVED
+```
+
+Documentation synchronization is never automatic.
+
+## 7. Directory-governance check
+
+Run:
+
+```bash
+python tests/check_repository_governance.py
+```
+
+before pushing structural changes.
+
+The check must fail when legacy documentation/data locations are reintroduced.
+
+## 8. GitHub protection
+
+`.github/CODEOWNERS` marks protected core assets as owner-reviewed paths.
+
+`CODEOWNERS` identifies the project owner for protected paths.
+If the repository later has more than one trusted reviewer, a default-branch
+ruleset may require Code Owner review. Do not enable a rule that prevents the
+sole repository owner from merging their own approved maintenance work.
